OSKit Arch — aarch64 CPU Primitives
===================================

Architecture-specific CPU operations for aarch64: interrupt
enable/disable, plus (eventually) spinlock helpers and stack-frame
construction. Mirrors the shape of `oskit/arch/x86_64/cpu.lsysl`
so the kernel can import from `oskit.arch` without caring which
target is underneath.

Implementations live in `cpu_asm.s` so we can emit bare `msr`
instructions without paying for a sysl function prologue.

    module oskit.arch

    extern arch_vm_copy_to_demand(dst_ptbr: u64, dst_vaddr: u64, src: *byte, len: int) -> int

Offset from the saved SP_EL1 to the syscall return register (x0).
On aarch64 the Linux syscall ABI puts the return value in x0, and
our 272-byte exception frame stores x0 at offset 0 (`[sp, #0x000]`).

    val SYSCALL_RETURN_OFFSET = 0
    val STACK_FRAME_SIZE = 272        // 0x110 — matches vectors.s save/restore

Frame offsets used by `execve` (and any future exec-shape primitive) to
overwrite the calling thread's saved program-counter / user-stack slots.
ELR_EL1 lives at 0x108 and SP_EL0 at 0xF8 inside the 272-byte EL0 frame
laid down by vectors.s and `build_user_stack_frame` below. Declared as
`const` so the compiler folds the literal at every use site rather than
emitting an indirect `ldr` through a global slot — module-level `val`
ints in sysl reach the .data section and the slot can briefly read 0
before `kernel_init` writes it; `const` is the deterministic fix.

    const ARCH_FRAME_PC_OFFSET  = 0x108
    const ARCH_FRAME_USP_OFFSET = 0xF8

Program + server address layout. User programs execute in the
per-process L3 carve-out at VA 0x60000000..0x6008FFFF (576KB):
  code  0x60000000..0x6007FFFF  (128 pages, 512KB)
  stack 0x60080000..0x6008FFFF  (16 pages, 64KB)
The default per-process VMA layout (`VMA_DEFAULT_*` in
`oskit/kernel/vma.lsysl`) is the single source of truth for these
ranges; PM and RS query the initial-SP / stack-low via the
`svc_vma_default_*` helpers (syscalls 96/97). `prog_config.sysl`
keeps only the bits a standalone user-program build needs —
PLATFORM_NAME + MMIO grant constants — without dragging
cpu.lsysl's kernel externs into the prog build.

Mask async exceptions (IRQ, FIQ, SError). Runs one instruction
plus the return; safe to call with interrupts already masked.

    extern arch_cli()

Unmask async exceptions.

    extern arch_sti()

Transition from EL1 to EL0 and start executing at `entry` with
`SP_EL0 = sp`. Does not return — an exception back to EL1 is the
way out. Caller must ensure the active page table maps both the
entry point and the user stack EL0-accessible.

    extern drop_to_el0(entry: i64, sp: i64)

Clean the D-cache line containing `addr` to PoU, then invalidate
the I-cache and ISB. Required after writing instruction bytes via
data stores so subsequent instruction fetch sees the new code.
Short form — fine for a single cache line (64B on A72).

    extern sync_icache_line(addr: i64)

Invalidate the entire instruction cache to PoU and flush the prefetch
pipeline. Used by `sys_execve_finalize_handler` so the calling thread
fetches the new program's instructions on its next ring-3 transition
rather than stale bytes the I-cache may still hold from the freed
predecessor. (PIPT I-cache caches by PA, and the freed frames are
quite likely to be exactly the ones being recycled.)

    extern arch_invalidate_icache_all()

Loop `dc cvau` over every 64-byte line in `[start, start+len)`, then
invalidate the whole I-cache and ISB. Reserved for future code-paths
that need to flush a known sub-range without the cost of touching the
entire I-cache.

    extern arch_sync_icache_range(start: i64, len: int)

Spinlock acquire/release (LDXR/STXR exclusive monitor). `acquire`
blocks until it wins the exclusive store and masks async exceptions
on success; `release` drops the word back to zero and unmasks.

    extern arch_spinlock_acquire(lock: *i64)
    extern arch_spinlock_release(lock: *i64)

Restore a 272-byte EL0 exception frame at SP_EL1 = `frame_sp`, then
ERET. Matches the save layout in `vectors.s`. Does not return.

    extern arch_resume_process(frame_sp: i64)

The exit trampoline every new thread initially returns into; on
aarch64 it's a simple jump into `thread_exit` from boot assembly
once the kernel defines it. Referenced by `build_stack_frame` so
that a kernel thread's entry function can simply "return" and end
up inside the exit trampoline.

    extern thread_exit: byte

Per-CPU kernel stack top pointer. On aarch64 the CPU does not auto-
swap SP_EL1 on EL0→EL1 — after an ERET the current SP_EL1 remains
the one we set before the eret. That's exactly what we want for the
next trap. So we store the value the scheduler last picked and make
`tss_set_rsp0` a no-op write that future SMP work can re-use.

    var current_kstack_top: i64 = 0

    tss_set_rsp0(rsp0: i64)
        current_kstack_top = rsp0

Build a fake EL1→EL1 exception frame on the supervisor stack so
that `arch_resume_process` can restore it and ERET into a fresh
kernel thread. Layout matches vectors.s (272 bytes):

  [0x000] x0, x1           [0x080] x16, x17
  [0x010] x2, x3           [0x090] x18, x19
  [0x020] x4, x5           [0x0A0] x20, x21
  [0x030] x6, x7           [0x0B0] x22, x23
  [0x040] x8, x9           [0x0C0] x24, x25
  [0x050] x10, x11         [0x0D0] x26, x27
  [0x060] x12, x13         [0x0E0] x28, x29
  [0x070] x14, x15         [0x0F0] x30, SP_EL0
                           [0x100] SPSR_EL1, ELR_EL1

`x30` is pre-loaded with `thread_exit` so a thread that `ret`s
from its entry function drops into the exit trampoline. SPSR_EL1
is set to `0x5` (M=EL1h, DAIF clear) so ERET stays at EL1 with
interrupts enabled; ELR_EL1 = entry_point.

    build_stack_frame(entry_point: i64, usp: i64, ssp: i64) -> i64
        val frame = ssp - STACK_FRAME_SIZE
        var i = 0
        while i < STACK_FRAME_SIZE
            var b: *byte = *byte(frame + i64(i))
            *b = byte(0)
            i += 1
        var x30_p: *i64 = *i64(frame + i64(0xF0))
        *x30_p = i64(&thread_exit)
        var sp0_p: *i64 = *i64(frame + i64(0xF8))
        *sp0_p = usp
        var spsr_p: *i64 = *i64(frame + i64(0x100))
        *spsr_p = i64(0x5)           // M=EL1h, DAIF clear
        var elr_p: *i64 = *i64(frame + i64(0x108))
        *elr_p = entry_point
        frame

Same shape as build_stack_frame but the frame targets EL0 on ERET:
SPSR_EL1 = 0 (M=EL0t, DAIF clear), SP_EL0 = usp. The exit trampoline
is NOT written to x30; user code that "returns" from its entry point
should instead issue the SYS_EXIT syscall. Caller (create_process_
suspended) handles thread_exit placement separately.

    build_user_stack_frame(entry_point: i64, usp: i64, ssp: i64) -> i64
        val frame = ssp - STACK_FRAME_SIZE
        var i = 0
        while i < STACK_FRAME_SIZE
            var b: *byte = *byte(frame + i64(i))
            *b = byte(0)
            i += 1
        var sp0_p: *i64 = *i64(frame + i64(0xF8))
        *sp0_p = usp
        var spsr_p: *i64 = *i64(frame + i64(0x100))
        *spsr_p = i64(0)             // M=EL0t, DAIF clear
        var elr_p: *i64 = *i64(frame + i64(0x108))
        *elr_p = entry_point
        frame

Set up the initial context frame for a new user process. On aarch64
the per-process kernel stack is identity-mapped (lives in the
Normal RAM block so it's reachable under any PT that mirrors the
kernel's L1[1] carve-out). Build the frame directly on `kstack_top_val`
— no cross-address-space copy needed. Returns the SSP value (frame
base) the scheduler will use as SP_EL1 on resume.

    arch_setup_process_frame(entry_point: i64, usp: i64, ssp: i64,
                             kstack_top_val: i64, ptbr_val: u64) -> i64
        build_user_stack_frame(entry_point, usp, kstack_top_val)
        kstack_top_val - i64(STACK_FRAME_SIZE)

Build a `fork()` continuation frame on the child's kernel stack.
Mirror of the x86_64 helper: byte-copy the parent's saved 272-byte
exception frame from `parent_ssp` onto the child's kstack, then
zero the syscall return register (x0 at offset 0) in the copy so
the child sees rc=0 from `sys_fork` while the parent sees the
child's pid (written by the dispatcher into the parent's saved x0
when `kernel_fork` returns).

ELR_EL1 in the copy points to the parent's post-SVC instruction;
SP_EL0 is the parent's user SP. Both arches identity-map the kstack
slab under any PTBR, so the copy succeeds in the kernel context
without an explicit address-space switch.

Returns the SSP value to store in the child thread (frame base).

    arch_setup_fork_frame(parent_ssp: i64, kstack_top_val: i64) -> i64
        val frame = kstack_top_val - i64(STACK_FRAME_SIZE)
        var i = 0
        while i < STACK_FRAME_SIZE
            var src: *byte = *byte(parent_ssp + i64(i))
            var dst: *byte = *byte(frame + i64(i))
            *dst = *src
            i += 1
        var rv: *i64 = *i64(frame + i64(SYSCALL_RETURN_OFFSET))
        *rv = i64(0)
        frame


`arch_trap_at_user_mode` reports whether the trap frame at `ssp`
records an EL0 entry (returns 1) or a kernel-mode entry (returns
0). The signal walker uses this to gate handler delivery: a thread
that took an SVC from kernel mode (eg. `sleep_or_notify` called by
`sys_ppoll`) has a nested current-EL trap frame on top, and arming
a sigframe there would ERET back into EL1 with PC set to a user
handler — an immediate instruction abort. Skipping delivery on
such frames defers the handler until the syscall unwinds back to
the original EL0-entry trap frame at the bottom of the kstack.

    arch_trap_at_user_mode(ssp: i64) -> int
        val pstate_p: *i64 = *i64(ssp + i64(0x100))
        val pstate = *pstate_p
        if (pstate & i64(0xF)) == i64(0)
            return 1
        0


Signal-frame construction for POSIX signal delivery
---------------------------------------------------

`arch_build_sigframe` lays down a `siginfo_t` + `ucontext_t` pair
on the target thread's user stack and rewrites the saved EL0 trap
frame so that the next ERET returns into the userspace signal
handler with `x0 = signo`, `x1 = &siginfo`, `x2 = &ucontext`,
`x30 = restorer`, and SP_EL0 pointing at the new frame. The
handler's `ret` lands on the restorer (musl's `__restore_rt`),
which issues SYS_RT_SIGRETURN to undo the frame.

User-side frame layout (the byte image we push onto the stack):

```
new_sp + 0     siginfo_t (128B, only si_signo non-zero)
new_sp + 128   ucontext_t (4544B):
                 +0    uc_flags          (u64, 0)
                 +8    uc_link           (u64, 0)
                 +16   uc_stack          (24B, zeroed)
                 +40   uc_sigmask        (128B; low 8B = saved
                                            kernel-shape sigmask)
                 +168  uc_mcontext (4376B):
                         +0    fault_address  (0)
                         +8    regs[31]       (x0..x30 from trap frame)
                         +256  sp             (saved SP_EL0)
                         +264  pc             (saved ELR_EL1)
                         +272  pstate         (saved SPSR_EL1)
                         +280  __reserved     (4096B, zeroed — no
                                                  FP/SIMD save yet)
```

Returns the new blocked-signal mask. The caller stores it into
`threads[tid].sigmask` and clears the pending bit. The 4672-byte
staging buffer lives at module scope; SLIX is single-CPU and the
walker is non-reentrant.

Saved-frame reads/writes go through the kernel's identity mapping
of the kstack: every page table SLIX builds mirrors the L1[1]
kernel slot, so `ssp + offset` is reachable directly from kernel
context regardless of which user PTBR is active. User-stack
writes go through `vm_copy_to`, which strides per-page via
`vm_v2p` internally.

    var _sigframe_buf: [4672]byte

    arch_build_sigframe(ssp: i64, ptbr: u64, signo: int,
                        handler: i64, restorer: i64,
                        sa_mask: i64, sa_flags: i32,
                        old_sigmask: i64) -> i64
        val saved_sp_p:     *i64 = *i64(ssp + i64(0xF8))
        val saved_pc_p:     *i64 = *i64(ssp + i64(0x108))
        val saved_pstate_p: *i64 = *i64(ssp + i64(0x100))
        val saved_sp     = *saved_sp_p
        val saved_pc     = *saved_pc_p
        val saved_pstate = *saved_pstate_p

        var z = 0
        while z < 4672
            _sigframe_buf[z] = byte(0)
            z += 1

        _sigframe_buf[0] = byte(signo & 0xFF)
        _sigframe_buf[1] = byte((signo >> 8) & 0xFF)
        _sigframe_buf[2] = byte((signo >> 16) & 0xFF)
        _sigframe_buf[3] = byte((signo >> 24) & 0xFF)

        var bi = 0
        while bi < 8
            val sh = i64(bi * 8)
            _sigframe_buf[168 + bi] = byte((old_sigmask >> sh) & i64(0xFF))
            bi += 1

        var r = 0
        while r < 248
            val src: *byte = *byte(ssp + i64(r))
            _sigframe_buf[304 + r] = *src
            r += 1

        var si = 0
        while si < 8
            val sh = i64(si * 8)
            _sigframe_buf[552 + si] = byte((saved_sp     >> sh) & i64(0xFF))
            _sigframe_buf[560 + si] = byte((saved_pc     >> sh) & i64(0xFF))
            _sigframe_buf[568 + si] = byte((saved_pstate >> sh) & i64(0xFF))
            si += 1

        val new_sp = (saved_sp - i64(4672)) & ~i64(15)
        arch_vm_copy_to_demand(ptbr, u64(new_sp), &_sigframe_buf[0], 4672)

        val siginfo_addr = new_sp
        val uctx_addr = new_sp + i64(128)

        var x0_p:  *i64 = *i64(ssp + i64(0x000))
        var x1_p:  *i64 = *i64(ssp + i64(0x008))
        var x2_p:  *i64 = *i64(ssp + i64(0x010))
        var x30_p: *i64 = *i64(ssp + i64(0x0F0))
        var sp_p:  *i64 = *i64(ssp + i64(0x0F8))
        var pc_p:  *i64 = *i64(ssp + i64(0x108))
        *x0_p  = i64(signo)
        *x1_p  = siginfo_addr
        *x2_p  = uctx_addr
        *x30_p = restorer
        *sp_p  = new_sp
        *pc_p  = handler

        var new_mask = old_sigmask | sa_mask
        if (sa_flags & i32(0x40000000)) == i32(0)
            new_mask = new_mask | (i64(1) << (signo - 1))
        new_mask


Sigreturn — restore a user trap frame from `ucontext`
-----------------------------------------------------

Inverse of `arch_build_sigframe`. The user just returned from a
signal handler via libc's `__restore_rt`, which issues SYS_RT_
SIGRETURN. At that point SP_EL0 is back at the `siginfo`/`ucontext`
pair we previously laid down — siginfo at sp+0, ucontext at sp+128
(handlers don't shift sp before calling the restorer because lr
already points at `__restore_rt`).

`arch_restore_sigframe` reads x1..x30, sp_el0, elr_el1, and
spsr_el1 out of `uc_mcontext` and writes them back into the
saved EL0 trap frame so the next ERET resumes at the saved PC
with the saved register state. `x0` is **not** written into the
trap frame — it is returned to the caller and the shim dispatcher
performs the usual "store syscall result" write, which lands the
restored x0 in its trap-frame slot without a double-write.

`out_sigmask` receives `uc_sigmask` (low 8 kernel-shape bytes);
the caller copies it into `threads[tid].sigmask`.

    arch_restore_sigframe(ssp: i64, ptbr: u64, out_sigmask: *i64) -> i64
        val user_sp_p: *i64 = *i64(ssp + i64(0xF8))
        val user_sp = *user_sp_p
        val uctx_va = user_sp + i64(128)

        var sigmask_bytes: [8]byte
        vm_copy_from(ptbr, u64(uctx_va + i64(40)), &sigmask_bytes[0], 8)
        val smp: *i64 = *i64(i64(&sigmask_bytes[0]))
        *out_sigmask = *smp

        var regbuf: [248]byte
        vm_copy_from(ptbr, u64(uctx_va + i64(176)), &regbuf[0], 248)

        var trio: [24]byte
        vm_copy_from(ptbr, u64(uctx_va + i64(424)), &trio[0], 24)
        val new_sp_p:     *i64 = *i64(i64(&trio[0]))
        val new_pc_p:     *i64 = *i64(i64(&trio[8]))
        val new_pstate_p: *i64 = *i64(i64(&trio[16]))

        val regbase = i64(&regbuf[0])
        var r = 1
        while r <= 30
            val src_p: *i64 = *i64(regbase + i64(r * 8))
            var dst_p: *i64 = *i64(ssp + i64(r * 8))
            *dst_p = *src_p
            r += 1

        var sp_w_p:   *i64 = *i64(ssp + i64(0xF8))
        var spsr_w_p: *i64 = *i64(ssp + i64(0x100))
        var elr_w_p:  *i64 = *i64(ssp + i64(0x108))
        *sp_w_p   = *new_sp_p
        *spsr_w_p = *new_pstate_p
        *elr_w_p  = *new_pc_p

        val x0_p: *i64 = *i64(regbase)
        *x0_p
